  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Very humid with locally heavy rainfall possible \nthis afternoon\n\nMorning rounds of rain have slid off to the southeast with only\nisolated showers in the northern tier of counties across our \nCWA. Afternoon coverage of showers and thunderstorms remains \nuncertain, but with additional convection beginning to develop \nto our west over Pittsburg's area we can expect at least a few \nheavy downpours later this afternoon before the stratiform rain\nshield over Ohio arrives later tonight. By midnight the cold \nfront should be south of the PA/MD border, and with some \nscattering of clouds anticipated and light winds leading into \nFriday morning fog development seems probable over areas that \nsaw rain today.\n\nPrevious Discussion... \nHighly anomalous moisture for this time of year (NAEFS mean \npwat values near climatological maximum percentiles) pooling \naround the periphery of subtropical high over the southern \nPlains and lower MS Valley will interact with a surface cold \nfront moving slowly south/southeastward from the lower Great \nLakes to produce periods of showers and a few locally heavy \nthunderstorms across Pennsylvania later today. 17/00Z HREF \nindicates the potential for spot rain amounts of 2-3 inches. \nIt's very likely that most of central PA picks up some rainfall \nby tonight. However, we do not anticipate an all-day \nrain/washout. Cloud trends remain a tough call with early \nmorning convection over north central PA, but fcst instability \nthis afternoon across south central PA will be more than \nsupportive for thunderstorm development. SPC continues to \nmaintain a general (non-severe) convective outlook for today \nwhich is likely due to the lack of deep layer shear (displaced \nfarther to the north) and poor mid level lapse rates (very \nmoist, tall/skinny soundings with warm cloud depths greater than\n10kft). \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Cooler this weekend with rain returning by Sunday\n\nAnother taste of Fall is on the way for the end of the week \nwith noticeably cooler and much drier/less humid air poised to \nreturn behind the front (Td crashing into the 40s/50s). Expect a\ncrisp and cool overnight Friday into Saturday morning with some\nupper 30s possible across the northern tier. Dry/fair wx \nconditions are forecast to start the weekend and last through \nthe day on Saturday followed by periods of potentially heavy \nrainfall Saturday night through early next week. Both NAEFS and\nECMWF SATs show at least 90th percentile PWs returning the the\nregion which is supportive for at least a marginal excessive\nrain signal. A surface low expected to track over the Great\nLakes will usher in this moisture and more than enough forcing\nfor widespread precipitation. NBM temps have also \ncooled/moderated quite a bit Mon- Wed with most locations \nstruggling to exceed 70F for daytime highs.",
